diff --git a/src/tensorfold/families/glm5_next/cuda/fastpf.py b/src/tensorfold/families/glm5_next/cuda/fastpf.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9967d41
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/tensorfold/families/glm5_next/cuda/fastpf.py
@@ -0,0 +1,179 @@
+"""Fast prefill for GLM-5.3-Flash on CUDA (patches/0080, GLM53_TF_FAST_PREFILL=1): prefill chunks through kernels
+that need not be row-invariant, with the engine's exactness guarantees kept by a chunk-grid rule.
+
+Why the decode kernels are row-invariant, and why prefill does not have to be
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
+Every decode and verify window runs row-invariant kernels: a row's bits never depend on its window-mates. That is
+what makes a drafted reply byte-identical to serial decoding (a verify row = the serial step at its position) and a
+resumed prompt identical to a fresh prefill (any chunking of a prompt gives the same state). It forbids big-tile
+GEMMs, split-K choices by row count, chunked (parallel) KDA scans and low-precision gathers for prefill.
+
+The guarantees only constrain prefill this much:
+
+(a) drafted == serial. Both decodings of a request start from the state its prefill left. Any DETERMINISTIC prefill
+    does: the same prompt gives the same state, and every decode window after it stays row-invariant (unchanged).
+(b) resumed == fresh. A prompt resumed from a kept snapshot must end in the state a fresh prefill of the whole
+    prompt gives. The fast kernels' bits may depend on a chunk's shape (its rows, its length, where its KDA sub-chunks
+    start), so the fresh prefill and the resumed one must cut the prompt into the same chunks from the snapshot on,
+    and the snapshot must hold what the fresh prefill holds at that position.
+
+The rule (``grid``, ``decode._prefill``, ``engine._run``)
+--------------------------------------------------------
+Let C = the request's prefill chunk rows rounded down to a multiple of 64 (at least 64), the fast chunk grid.
+
+1. Chunks at absolute multiples of C. A fast prefill of tokens t[0:n] starting at position b (b = 0, or a resume
+   point, itself a multiple of C) runs the chunks [kC, min((k+1)C, n)) for kC >= b. A chunk's computation is a
+   deterministic function of (its absolute start kC, its length, its tokens, the committed state before kC): the fast
+   kernels are deterministic (no atomics, fixed tile and reduction orders), KDA sub-chunks start at multiples of 64
+   (C is one), and everything else a chunk reads (attention caches, indexer caches) holds positions < kC only.
+2. A fast snapshot sits on the grid. Of a fast prefill of n tokens only the state at B = floor(n / C) * C is kept
+   (``Snapshot.ids = t[:B]``, tagged ``grid = C``): before the last, partial, chunk [B, n) runs, or after the prefill
+   when B = n. Positions >= B are never kept: the partial chunk's shape depends on n, and the reply's rows were
+   written by decode windows (row-invariant kernels), neither of which a fresh prefill of a longer prompt reproduces.
+3. A fast request resumes only from a snapshot with its own grid C (exact requests only from exact snapshots,
+   grid 0), and re-prefills from B: the tail of the old prompt past B, the old reply and the new tokens, in the
+   chunks of rule 1.
+
+Claim: under rules 1-3, a fast prefill of prompt p resumed from snapshot S (grid C, len(S.ids) = B, p[:B] = S.ids)
+ends in the state a fresh fast prefill of p ends in, bit for bit (every piece of committed state: KDA recurrent
+states and conv windows, attention and indexer caches, the MTP head's cache and pending row, DFlash2's cache).
+
+Lemma. Two fresh fast prefills (same grid C) of prompts that share their first B tokens (B a multiple of C, both at
+least B long) hold equal state at position B. Every chunk [kC, (k+1)C) with (k+1)C <= B is full in both (same
+shape, rule 1) and reads equal inputs: the same tokens, and by induction on k the same committed state before kC.
+
+Proof of the claim, by induction over the prefills that made the snapshots (each one fresh, or resumed from an
+earlier snapshot of the same grid). Let P0 be the prefill that took S, of prompt q with q[:B] = S.ids = p[:B]. By the
+induction hypothesis (or trivially when P0 was fresh) P0 ran exactly the chunks a fresh prefill of q runs from its
+resume point on, from the state a fresh prefill of q holds there, so at position B it held the fresh prefill of q's
+state, which by the lemma is the fresh prefill of p's state at B. S copied the parts later requests overwrite (KDA
+states and conv windows; the pending MTP row B-1, the main model's final-normed row from the full chunk ending at B,
+with ``mtp_len = B - 1``; DFlash2's ``context_end = B``) and left the caches at positions < B in place; no request
+touched those since: a request writes caches only from its own resume point on, and ``engine._run`` drops every
+snapshot longer than that point before the prefill. So restoring S gives the fresh prefill of p's state at B, and
+from B on the resumed and the fresh prefill run the same chunks (rule 1: B is a multiple of C) on equal inputs,
+including the last chunk whose logits give the first token. The MTP head's absorb and DFlash2's taps stay on the
+row-invariant kernels, so their caches depend only on the main model's rows (equal) and not on how the rows were
+batched; the resumed prefill re-absorbs position B - 1 with the next token p[B], exactly what the fresh one did. QED.
+
+With (a) on top: a drafted request that resumed from a fast snapshot starts decoding from the fresh fast prefill's
+state, which is what the ``draft: false`` reference (always a fresh prefill) starts from.
+
+What a turn costs: the next turn of a conversation re-prefills the old prompt's tail past B (< C tokens) and the
+old reply through the fast path, then the new tokens. Exact snapshots after a reply (the row-invariant mode) avoid
+that, which is why the switch is per request (``tf_knobs.fast_prefill``, patches/0091) and snapshots carry their
+grid.
+
+What runs fast (only the main model's prefill chunks; decode, verify, the MTP head and DFlash2 never)
+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+- routed experts (EXL3): ``exl3_fast.cu`` (fused grouped GEMMs, no Z partials), in-tree;
+- KDA: ``fast_kda.kda_prefill_chunked`` (chunked delta rule, sub-chunks at absolute multiples of 64), when present;
+- non-expert 4-bit / BF16 matmuls: ``fast_qmm.matmul_fast`` (large-M tiles, one accumulator over K), when present,
+  through ``qmm.matmul``;
+- hyper-connection mixing dots (``glue.hc_pre``): ``fast_qmm.hc_partial`` (row tiles share fn), when present;
+- rank partials: all-gathered as bf16 (GLM53_TF_FAST_GATHER=bf16, the default; fp32 keeps upstream's gather),
+  half the bytes on the link; both ranks add the same rounded partials, rank 0 first;
+- the head: only the last row of the last chunk (prefill samples one token; the MTP head reads the final-normed
+  rows, which are still computed for every row).
+
+``fast_kda`` and ``fast_qmm`` ship in patches/0081; without them those parts run the row-invariant kernels, and the
+rule above holds all the same (it asks nothing of the kernels but determinism).
+"""
+
+from __future__ import annotations
+
+import contextlib
+import os
+
+GRID = 64                 # the KDA sub-chunk: fast chunk boundaries are multiples of it
+
+MISSING: dict[str, str] = {}      # optional fast module -> why it is not used (reported at load by the engine)
+
+
+def _optional(name: str):
+    """patches/0081's modules when they are there; a module that is there but fails to import is reported (the
+    engine prints it when fast prefill is on) instead of silently leaving its part on the row-invariant kernels."""
+
+    import importlib
+
+    try:
+        return importlib.import_module(f"{__package__}.{name}")
+    except ModuleNotFoundError as exc:
+        if exc.name != f"{__package__}.{name}":
+            MISSING[name] = f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
+        else:
+            MISSING[name] = "not installed (patches/0081)"
+    except Exception as exc:      # noqa: BLE001 - a broken optional module must not break the exact engine
+        MISSING[name] = f"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}"
+    return None
+
+
+fast_kda = _optional("fast_kda")      # the chunked KDA scan
+fast_qmm = _optional("fast_qmm")      # large-M 4-bit / BF16 matmuls
+
+
+def enabled() -> bool:
+    """GLM53_TF_FAST_PREFILL (default 0): prefill chunks through the fast kernels by default."""
+
+    return os.environ.get("GLM53_TF_FAST_PREFILL", "0").strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes", "on")
+
+
+def gather16() -> bool:
+    """GLM53_TF_FAST_GATHER=bf16 (default) | fp32: the dtype of the rank partials a fast chunk all-gathers."""
+
+    v = os.environ.get("GLM53_TF_FAST_GATHER", "bf16").strip().lower()
+    if v not in ("bf16", "fp32"):
+        raise ValueError(f"GLM53_TF_FAST_GATHER={v!r}: expected bf16 or fp32")
+    return v == "bf16"
+
+
+def grid(rows: int) -> int:
+    """The fast chunk grid C for a chunk size of ``rows``: rows rounded down to a multiple of 64, at least 64."""
+
+    return max(GRID, (int(rows) // GRID) * GRID)
+
+
+def settings() -> list[int]:
+    """This rank's load-time fast-prefill settings (both ranks must agree: they decide the prefill's bits)."""
+
+    return [int(enabled()), int(gather16()), int(fast_kda is not None), int(fast_qmm is not None)]
+
+
+@contextlib.contextmanager
+def chunk(b):
+    """A fast chunk of the main model on buffers ``b``: the blocks see ``b.fast``, ``qmm.matmul`` runs
+    ``fast_qmm.matmul_fast`` and ``glue.hc_pre`` its dots through ``fast_qmm.hc_partial`` (when present) until the
+    chunk's forward returns."""
+
+    from . import glue, qmm
+
+    b.fast = True
+    prev, prev_hc = qmm.FAST_MM, glue.FAST_HC
+    mm = None if fast_qmm is None else getattr(fast_qmm, "matmul_fast", None) or fast_qmm.matmul_prefill
+    qmm.FAST_MM = mm
+    glue.FAST_HC = getattr(fast_qmm, "hc_partial", None)
+    try:
+        yield
+    finally:
+        qmm.FAST_MM, glue.FAST_HC = prev, prev_hc
+        b.fast = False
+
+
+def on(b) -> bool:
+    return getattr(b, "fast", False)
+
+
+def kda_chain(p, b_off, a, g, conv_state, conv_w, state_in, a_log, dt_bias, norm_w, eps, lower, rows, scratch,
+              state_out, *, pos: int):
+    """KDA for a fast chunk starting at absolute position ``pos`` (a multiple of 64): ``fast_kda`` when present,
+    else the row-invariant chain. Same inputs, outputs and state layout as ``kda.chain`` (state [H, 128 (v), 128 (k)]
+    fp32; the conv window is shifted by ``forward.commit`` from the projection rows, as always). The replay inputs in
+    ``scratch`` are not needed: a prefill chunk keeps all its rows."""
+
+    if fast_kda is not None:
+        return fast_kda.kda_prefill_chunked(p, b_off, a, g, conv_state, conv_w, state_in, a_log, dt_bias, norm_w, eps,
+                                            lower, rows, scratch, state_out, pos=pos)
+    from . import kda as kda_mod
+
+    return kda_mod.chain(p, b_off, a, g, conv_state, conv_w, state_in, a_log, dt_bias, norm_w, eps, lower, rows,
+                         scratch, state_out)
